const description = "Freezer running but not freezing, or not cold enough? The usual causes are a frosted-over defrost system, dirty condenser coils, or a stalled fan. What to check and when it's a tech job.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my freezer running but not freezing?",
    a: "The freezer is powered and the light works, but nothing stays frozen — that almost always means cold air is being made but not moving, or not being made at all. The three usual culprits are a defrost system that has iced over and choked the evaporator coil, a stalled evaporator fan that can't push cold air into the compartment, or dirty condenser coils that keep the compressor from pulling heat out. A door seal that no longer closes tight comes next. A true compressor or sealed-system failure is real but less common than people assume, which is good news, because the frequent causes are far cheaper to fix."
  },
  {
    q: "What temperature should a freezer be?",
    a: "0°F (about −18°C) is the standard target for a home freezer, and it's the point at which food stays safely frozen long-term. If your freezer is holding 10°F or 20°F, food will look frozen but slowly degrade and the unit is telling you something is wrong. Set the freezer to 0°F, give it 24 hours, and check with a cheap appliance thermometer. If it won't hold 0°F no matter the setting, that's a diagnostic, not a dial problem."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a freezer that isn't cold enough myself?",
    a: "Some of it, yes, and it's worth trying before you call anyone. Vacuum the condenser coils, make sure the door seals fully and isn't blocked by an overpacked shelf, confirm the interior vents aren't buried under food, and give the unit a few inches of breathing room from the wall. If it's a manual-defrost or frost-choked unit, unplugging it and letting it thaw fully for a day often restores it. What you should not do is open the sealed refrigeration system, test the start relay or capacitor, or handle refrigerant — those are where our techs take over."
  },
  {
    q: "Why did my garage freezer stop freezing in the heat?",
    a: "This is common in Southern California summers. A freezer in a hot garage has to fight the ambient temperature, and once the garage climbs past the mid-90s, a unit with even slightly dirty condenser coils or a marginal compressor can't keep up. The compressor runs constantly and still loses ground. Cleaning the coils and improving airflow around the unit helps a lot; if it still can't hold 0°F on a hot afternoon, the cooling system is near the end of its capacity and worth having looked at."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing a freezer that won't freeze?",
    a: "It depends on the part and the age. A standalone freezer typically lasts 10 to 20 years (NAHB life-expectancy data). If a 6-year-old unit needs a defrost heater, a fan motor, or a start relay, repair is an easy yes — those run a fraction of a new freezer. If a 16-year-old unit needs a compressor or has a sealed-system leak, the repair can approach the cost of replacement, and we'll tell you that honestly rather than sell you a repair that doesn't make sense. Our diagnostic pins down which situation you're in for $89, waived with the repair."
  }
];

